The symmetric part of the tensor $\mathbf{K}_s$ consists of a parallel diffusion coefficient ($K_{\parallel}$) and a perpendicular diffusion coefficient ($K_{\perp}$), whereas the antisymmetric element $K_A$ describes gradient and curvature drifts in the large scale HMF. The pitch-angle-averaged guiding center drift velocity for a near-isotropic CR distribution is given by $\langle \mathbf{v}_D \rangle = \nabla \times [K_A e_B]$, where $e_B = B/B$, with $B$ the magnitude of the background HMF. This equation was solved numerically in a steady state ($\partial f / \partial t = 0$) and by assuming azimuthal symmetry ($\partial f / \partial \phi = 0$). Both assumptions are reasonable because this work is restricted to studying global modulation during minimum modulation periods when relatively fast large scale changes do not occur in the heliosphere. The details of this particular two-dimensional (2-D) model and the manner in which the current sheet drifts were handled, the so-called WNS model, were described by Burger and Hattingh [1995]. The basic characteristics of this model are similar to the well-known model of Potgieter and Moraal [1985]. Hattingh [1998] and Ferreira et al. [1999] compared the results of this 2-D model with that of a 3-D model, both developed by the modulation group in Potchefstroom. They concluded that because no qualitative differences were found, the use of a 2-D model was still well justified. Caution is required when one deviates from solar minimum conditions. The solar wind speed was assumed to change from 400 km s$^{-1}$ in the equatorial plane ($\theta = 90^{\circ}$) to a maximum of 800 km s$^{-1}$ when $\theta \le 60^{\circ}$ in qualitative agreement with observations [e.g., Phillips et al., 1995]. The outer boundary of the simulated heliosphere was assumed at 100 AU, which still seems a reasonable consensus value. The local interstellar spectrum (LIS) for protons in terms of differential intensity $j_E$ and kinetic energy $E$ was assumed as $j_E = 7.37 E^{0.7} / (E + 0.25)^{3.0}$ (W. R. Webber, private communication, 1999). The HMF magnitude, based on a Parker geometry, was modified only in the polar regions of the heliosphere according to Jokipii and Kóta [1989]; this modification is qualitatively supported by measurements of the HMF in the polar regions of the heliosphere on board Ulysses [Forsyth et al., 1995]. The so-called Fisk field [Fisk, 1996] was not considered because of its complex geometry and basic dynamic nature applicable only to the polar regions with a fast solar wind speed. When this is combined with the slow solar wind regions, the modeling requires an approach different than used for numerical modulation models so far (see, for <!-- page 3 -->
example, Kóta and Jokipii [1997, 1999]). Note that the concepts of enhanced perpendicular diffusion and the Fisk field as possible explanations for the persistence of the 26-day recurrent modulation at high heliolatitudes are both to some extent still controversial, as discussed by Fisk and Jokipii [1999]. For $K_{\parallel}$, $K_{\perp}$, and the "drift" coefficient $K_A$, the following general forms were assumed, respectively, for Figures 1 and 2: $$
\begin{aligned}
K_{\parallel} &= K_0 \beta f_1(R) f_2(\theta, r); & K_{\perp r} &= a K_{\parallel}; \\
K_{\perp \theta} &= b K_{\parallel}; & K_A &= (K_A)_0 \frac{\beta R}{3B}. \end{aligned} 
\quad (2)
$$ For Figures 3 and 4 they were assumed as $$
\begin{aligned}
K_{\parallel} &= K_0 \beta f_1(R) f_2(\theta, r); & K_{\perp r} &= a K_{\parallel}; \\
K_{\perp \theta} &= f_3(\theta) K_{\perp r}; & K_A &= (K_A)_0 \frac{\beta R}{3B}. \end{aligned} 
\quad (3)
$$ Here $r$ is radial distance and $\theta$ is polar angle, $\beta$ is the ratio of the speed of the CR particles to the speed of light; $f_1(R)$ gives the rigidity dependence in GV; $K_0$ is a constant in units of $10^{20} \text{ cm}^2 \text{ s}^{-1}$; and $a$ is a constant which determines the value of $K_{\perp r}$ which contributes to perpendicular diffusion in the radial direction. The effective radial diffusion coefficient is given by $K_{rr} = K_{\parallel} \cos^2 \psi + K_{\perp r} \sin^2 \psi$, with $\psi$ the angle between the radial direction and the averaged HMF direction. Note that $\psi \to 90^\circ$ when $r > \sim 10$ AU and with the polar angle $\theta \to 90^\circ$, but $\psi \to 0^\circ$ when $\theta \to 0^\circ$, and there are subsequent effects on $K_{rr}$. Diffusion perpendicular to the HMF was enhanced in the polar direction by assuming that $K_{\theta\theta} = K_{\perp\theta} = b K_{\parallel}$, with $b \ge a$ in equation (2), which implies that the enhancement of perpendicular diffusion in the polar direction is then considered the same throughout the heliosphere. (Note that because of the spherical geometry used, $K_{\theta\theta}$ is multiplied by $\cos \theta$ in the transport equation. ) Because this assumption may be too generalized, it was also assumed that $K_{\perp\theta} = f_3(\theta) K_{\perp r}$, with $f_3(\theta) = (b^* + 1)/2 + [(b^* - 1)/2] \tanh[(\theta - 90^\circ - 35^\circ)/0.125]$ (see also Hattingh [1998]). This function increases from unity in the equatorial plane ($90^\circ$) to a maximum value of $b^* > 1.0$ at the poles, with a half value at $35^\circ$. To obtain compatibility with the small latitude gradients measured with Ulysses, $b^*$ was found to be of the order of 10 to 20, which will be illustrated below. This means that $K_{\perp\theta}$ changes significantly only in the polar regions of the heliosphere as suggested by Kóta and Jokipii [1995] and in agreement with Ferrando et al. [1993]. The amount of drifts allowed is specified by $(K_A)_0$, with $(K_A)_0 = 1.0$ a maximum. The magnitude of the modified HMF is given by $B$. For $f_1(R)$, the damping model - composite slab - 2-D geometry of Bieber et al. [1994] was used; see also Potgieter [1996]. For the results given below, $f_1(R)$ was used to a lowest energy of 100 MeV. Values for the different constants and for the functions $f_2(\theta, r)$ and $f_3(\theta)$ are given with the respective results below. The "tilt angle" of the heliospheric current sheet was assumed to be $5^\circ$, which is associated with solar minimum modulation. The assumption of $K_{\perp r} \propto K_{\parallel}$ seems justified based on the recent computations of cosmic ray transport in a turbulent magnetic field by Giacalone et al. [1999]. They did not, however, comment on the relation between $K_{\perp\theta}$ and $K_{\parallel}$. It was found by Ferreira and Potgieter [1999], who worked on electron modulation, that $K_{\perp\theta} \propto K_{\parallel}$, with $f_1(R)$ as assumed in this paper, gave good compatibility with electron observations made on board the Ulysses spacecraft. In this paper the functions $f_1$ to $f_3$ are determined by the extent to which compatibility is obtained between model solutions and observations. They are not unique, and further justification for their functional forms will have to await a comprehensive theory of how these functions should vary with latitude, and even with radial distance. ## 3. Results The following observations were used as a guide to determine the compatibility between computations and measurements. The latitudinal variation expressed as the ratio of the equatorial to the polar counting rates for ~200 MeV protons observed by Ulysses for the present $A > 0$ cycle was taken to be a factor of 1.2 to 1.4 from Heber et al. [1996]. For the radial variation the 130-220 MeV proton intensities from IMP 8 at 1 AU and the Pioneer 10 and Voyager 1 and 2 observations between ~42 AU and ~64 AU for the solar minimum modulation periods in 1987 and 1996 were taken from Webber and Lockwood [1997]. These periods are so-called $A < 0$ and $A > 0$ HMF polarity epochs respectively. It was also taken into consideration that a relatively large and clearly negative latitudinal gradient was observed between Voyager 1 and 2 at ~30 AU in 1987 (see, for example, Lockwood and Webber [1992], and Potgieter and le Roux [1992]). Although restricted, the data set is quite adequate for the purpose of understanding the global features of heliospheric modulation. The final criterion was that each particular set of solutions had to fit reasonably the appropriate proton spectrum at Earth. The first results illustrate the computed radial and latitude dependence of the differential intensity for 200 MeV protons using $f_2(\theta, r) = (1 + r/r_0)$ in equation (2), where $r$ is radial distance, $r_0 = 1$ AU, $K_0 = 12$, $(K_A)_0 = 1.0$, and $a = b = 0.01$, which means that no enhancement of $K_{\perp\theta}$ was allowed. Further justification for using $f_2 \propto r$ has been given in Haasbroek and Potgieter [1995]. These results were done for the $A > 0$ polarity. The first objective here is to establish if the model with the given assumptions and with no enhancement of perpendicular diffusion could give a latitudinal effect similar to that observed in the inner heliosphere by Ulysses.

Finally, Nishida [1982, 1983] has performed thorough and detailed numerical calculations of Forbush decreases based on enhanced scattering and solar wind speed behind a propagating shock wave for both the convection-diffusion equation and the full transport equation including energy dependence. His calculated profiles bear a striking resemblance to observed profiles, including energy-dependent precursor, decrease, and recovery. There has been very little theoretical work on the 11-year time-dependent modulation. Nevertheless, there are two papers of importance. O'Gallagher [1975] developed a time-dependent theory based on the convection-diffusion transport equation to account for the hysteresis effect observed over the 11-year cycle. Although his approach was not systematic and direct, his expansion procedure based on the slow variation of the transport coefficients during the 11-year cycle established the foundation for a more systematic approach. Perko and Fisk [1983] solved numerically the full time-dependent spherically symmetric transport equation over a solar cycle based on the assumption that the cycle is caused by interplanetary disturbances which propagate through the heliosphere with a rate of occurrence which varies with the solar cycle. The disturbances were represented by spatially localized decreases in the radial diffusion coefficient. With reasonable decreases and occurrence rates they were able to reproduce the 11-year variation including the hysteresis effect. Although Perko and Fisk did not study the individual Forbush decreases in any detail, their hysteresis plot shows clearly the microstructure associated with Forbush decreases and recovery apparent in plots based on measurements. Their results support the theoretical feasibility of producing the 11-year variation from the cumulative effect of many Forbush decreases. It is the purpose of this paper to provide a simple but systematic analytical framework for treating time-dependent problems in cosmic ray modulation. The theory is a perturbation theory based on linearizing the standard transport equation in temporal variations of the transport coefficients and the resulting cosmic ray response. Although a perturbation approach cannot replace the fine numerical work of Nishida [1982, 1983] or Perko and Fisk [1983] which is well suited to the large-amplitude variations observed and a realistic spatial and energy dependence of the transport coefficients, it does provide a conceptual generality, simple analytical expressions, reproducibility, and perhaps even physical insights not possible in numerical work. After presenting the basic theory, in this initial paper we shall restrict ourselves to the time-dependent convection-diffusion equation (as in the works by Parker [1963], O'Gallagher [1975], and Nishida [1982]) which neglects particle energy changes in the divergent solar wind. We shall also neglect drift transport and consider only variations in the spatial diffusion coefficient and the resulting variations in the cosmic ray number density. Nevertheless, we will present a quantitative analytical theory of Forbush decreases and recovery, and of the 11-year cycle, which can account for a variety of observations. Energy-dependent inferences can be made by considering various magnitudes of the zeroth-order diffusion coefficient. In section 2 we present the basic equations. In section 3 we apply the theory based on the convection-diffusion equation to the 11-year variation, and in section 4 we apply it to For-bush decreases. In section 5 we summarize and discuss our results and outline extensions of the theory to a broader range of problems. # 2. BASIC EQUATIONS The fundamental equation for galactic cosmic ray transport in interplanetary space is [Parker, 1965; Gleeson and Axford, 1967; Jokipii et al., 1977] $$
\partial f / \partial t + \nabla \cdot \mathbf {S} + \frac {1}{3} p ^ {- 2} \partial / \partial p (\mathbf {V} \cdot \nabla p ^ {3} f) = 0 \tag {1}
$$ $$
\mathbf {S} = - \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla f + \boldsymbol {\Lambda} \times \nabla f - \frac {1}{3} \mathbf {V} p \partial f / \partial p \tag {2}
$$ where $f(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ is the cosmic ray omnidirectional distribution function (of a given species, which we take to be protons), $\mathbf{S}(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ is the streaming or differential flux, $p$ is momentum magnitude, $\mathbf{V}(\mathbf{r}, t)$ is the solar wind velocity, and $\mathbf{K}(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ is the symmetric part of the spatial diffusion tensor. The quantity $\Lambda$ , which describes drift transport, equals $pvc(3eB^{2})^{-1}\mathbf{B}$ , where $v$ is particle speed, $e$ is particle charge, and $\mathbf{B}$ is the interplanetary magnetic field. Equations (1) and (2) neglect terms proportional to $|\mathbf{V}|^{2}/v^{2}$ and are based on the diffusive approximation that the scattering mean free path is small in comparison with the scale length for spatial variation. Equations (1) and (2) must be solved subject to the standard boundary conditions that $f(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ match the interstellar spectrum at the modulation boundary $|\mathbf{r}| = R$ and that the sun supply no particles [Fisk, 1979]: $$
f (| \mathbf {r} | = R, p, t) = F (p) \tag {3}
$$ $$
\mathbf {S} (\mathbf {r} = 0, p, t) = 0 \tag {4}
$$ Although these particular boundary conditions are not fundamental (they neglect the effects of an absorbing sun and the detailed configuration of the distant heliosphere), we accept them for our purposes without question. In order to address transient variations we consider a stationary configuration and small time-dependent perturbations in the solar wind parameters and the cosmic ray distribution. We then write $f(\mathbf{r}, p, t) = f_0(\mathbf{r}, p) + \delta f(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ and $\mathbf{K}(\mathbf{r}, p, t) = \mathbf{K}_0(\mathbf{r}, p) + \delta \mathbf{K}(\mathbf{r}, p, t)$ with similar expressions for $\mathbf{V}$ and $\mathbf{V}_D \equiv \nabla \times \Lambda$ . Combining (1) and (2), the zeroth- and first-order equations in the perturbations are $$
- \nabla \cdot (\mathbf {K} _ {0} \cdot \nabla f _ {0}) + (\mathbf {V} _ {0} + \mathbf {V} _ {D, 0}) \cdot \nabla f _ {0} - \frac {1}{3} \nabla \cdot \mathbf {V} _ {0} p \partial f _ {0} / \partial p = 0 \tag {5}
$$ $$
\partial \delta f / \partial t - \nabla \cdot (\mathbf {K} _ {0} \cdot \nabla \delta f) + (\mathbf {V} _ {0} + \mathbf {V} _ {D, 0}) \cdot \nabla \delta f - \frac {1}{3} \nabla \cdot \mathbf {V} _ {0} p \partial \delta f / \partial p
$$ $$
= \nabla \cdot (\delta \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla f _ {0}) - (\delta \mathbf {V} + \delta \mathbf {V} _ {D}) \cdot \nabla f _ {0} + \frac {1}{3} \nabla \cdot \delta \mathbf {V} p \partial f _ {0} / \partial p \tag {6}
$$ subject to the boundary conditions $$
f _ {0} (| \mathbf {r} | = R _ {0}, p) = F (p) \tag {7}
$$ $$
\delta f (| \mathbf {r} | = R _ {0}, p, t) + (\partial f _ {0} / \partial | \mathbf {r} |) _ {| \mathbf {r} | = R _ {0}} \delta R (t) = 0 \tag {8}
$$ $$
[ - \mathbf {K} _ {0} \cdot \nabla f _ {0} + \mathbf {V} _ {D, 0} f _ {0} - \frac {1}{3} \mathbf {V} _ {0} p \partial f _ {0} / \partial p ] _ {\mathbf {r} = 0} = 0 \tag {9}
$$ $$
[ - \mathsf {K} _ {0} \cdot \nabla \delta f + \mathbf {V} _ {D, 0} \delta f - \frac {1}{3} \mathbf {V} _ {0} p \partial \delta f / \partial p ] _ {\mathbf {r} = 0}
$$ $$
= [ \delta \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla f _ {0} - \delta \mathbf {V} _ {D} f _ {0} + \frac {1}{3} \delta \mathbf {V} p \partial f _ {0} / \partial p ] _ {r = 0} \tag {10}
$$ where $\delta R(t)$ is the possible variation in the heliocentric distance of the outer boundary. The solution of (5), (7), and (9) yields the stationary distribution, $f_{0}(\mathbf{r}, p)$ . The solution of (6), (8), and (10) yields the linear response to small variations in the solar wind parameters. A simpler set of equations result if we can neglect the $p$ dependence of $K_{0}$ and $V_{D,0}$ , and if we integrate this set over p to obtain equations for the cosmic ray number density, $$
N (\mathbf {r}, t) = 4 \pi \int_ {0} ^ {\infty} d p p ^ {2} f (\mathbf {r}, p, t)
$$ If we further neglect drift transport and consider only variations in K, we obtain $$
- \nabla \cdot (\mathsf {K} _ {0} \cdot \nabla N _ {0}) + \nabla \cdot (\mathsf {V} N _ {0}) = 0 \tag {11}
